  "body": "[The spec](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#timers) specifies the algorithm of evaluating `setTimeout` invocations; the second parameter, `timeout`, is mentioned first at step 6:\n\n> 6. Let timeout be the second method argument.\n\nLater it compares `timeout` to various numbers:\n\n> 8. If nesting level is greater than 5, and timeout is less than 4, then increase timeout to 4.\n\nand speaks about \"`timeout` milliseconds\". I don't see anywhere that `timeout` has to be a number parameter nor a casting behavior seems specified.\n\nThe [W3C HTML5 spec for setTimeout](https://www.w3.org/TR/2011/WD-html5-20110525/timers.html) does specify the exact [casting algorithm for the `timeout` parameter](https://www.w3.org/TR/2011/WD-html5-20110525/timers.html#get-the-timeout). Why is not that the case for the WHATWG spec?\n\nOTH, if I'm reading the W3C spec correctly, according to it the following code:\n\n``` js\nsetTimeout(\n    () => console.log('end'),\n    {toString: () => '2000', valueOf: () => 3000}\n);\n```\n\nshould print `'end'` after 2 seconds and in Chrome & Firefox it prints it after 3 seconds.\n",
